This worker contains downstream source changes in Khua-playback-window.patch.
The patch adds authenticated, per-consumer playback retention windows, shares
forward cache capacity between windows, and evicts watched or unrelated whole
pieces independently of cache-capacity pressure. Playback windows do not start
downloads. Managed HTTP readers request data within the assigned bounds; ordinary
whole-file saves keep their separate reader behavior. Whole current/in-flight
pieces can cross a media-time cutoff; incomplete pieces are never zeroed and
misrepresented as completed content.
A live playback window also owns the paused torrent independently of HTTP
readers and idle status polling. Replacing a consumer window is atomic; only
explicit release removes that ownership. Idle managed readers park at EOF so
they retain no download priority and resume at their original logical offset.
Active managed HTTP readers start with 16 MiB of lookahead immediately, bounded
by the applied playback window; metadata reads outside it do not extend that
lookahead. Playback windows alone continue to request no missing data.
Managed foreground GETs with one finite byte range allow HTTP/1.1 connection
reuse. Each response still closes its torrent reader, and a client-requested
connection close is honored. Unmanaged and prefetch stream behavior is unchanged.

